window.__OPENSTAX_PHYSICS__[14] = {"schemaVersion":"4.0.0-physics","generatedAt":"2026-11-27T01:33:30.775Z","number":14,"code":"ch14","slug":"sound","title":"Sound","titleVi":"Sóng Âm & Cộng Hưởng (Sound)","sections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec00","moduleId":"m54327","number":"14.0","slug":"introduction","title":"Introduction","isIntroduction":true,"learningObjectives":[],"bilingualGuide":null,"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec00.sub01","title":"Chapter Introduction","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["If a tree falls in a forest (see [Figure 14.0]) and no one is there to hear it, does it make a sound? The answer to this old philosophical question depends on how you define sound. If sound only exists when someone is around to perceive it, then the falling tree produced no sound. However, in physics, we know that colliding objects can disturb the air, water or other matter surrounding them. As a result of the collision, the surrounding particles of matter began vibrating in a wave-like fashion. This is a sound wave. Consequently, if a tree collided with another object in space, no one would hear it, because no sound would be produced. This is because, in space, there is no air, water or other matter to be disturbed and produce sound waves. In this chapter, we’ll learn more about the wave properties of sound, and explore hearing, as well as some special uses for sound."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064897041","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_00_Tree.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_00_Tree.jpg","alt":"A fallen tree lies on the ground in a forest."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[],"summaryPoints":[],"glossary":[],"assessments":[],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":0,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":1,"workedExampleCount":0,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":0,"assessmentCount":0}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01","moduleId":"m54331","number":"14.1","slug":"speed-of-sound-frequency-and-wavelength","title":"Speed of Sound, Frequency, and Wavelength","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Relate the characteristics of waves to properties of sound waves","Describe the speed of sound and how it changes in various media","Relate the speed of sound to frequency and wavelength of a sound wave"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Bản chất sóng âm (sóng dọc), tốc độ âm thanh trong không khí (v ≈ 331 + 0.6T m/s) và liên hệ v = fλ.","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"speed of sound at 20 °C","meaningVi":"Tốc độ âm thanh ở 20 °C ≈ 343 m/s","mathNotation":"v ≈ 343 m/s"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Âm thanh truyền NHANH NHẤT trong chất rắn (thép ~5000 m/s), KHÔNG phải trong không khí!"]},"keyEquations":[{"name":"speed of sound","formula":"\\(v = f\\lambda\\)"}],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.sub01","title":"Properties of Sound Waves","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Sound is a wave. More specifically, sound is defined to be a disturbance of matter that is transmitted from its source outward. A disturbance is anything that is moved from its state of equilibrium. Some sound waves can be characterized as periodic waves, which means that the atoms that make up the matter experience simple harmonic motion .","A vibrating string produces a sound wave as illustrated in [Figure 14.1], [Figure 14.1], and [Figure 14.1]. As the string oscillates back and forth, part of the string’s energy goes into compressing and expanding the surrounding air. This creates slightly higher and lower pressures. The higher pressure... regions are compressions, and the low pressure regions are rarefactions . The pressure disturbance moves through the air as longitudinal waves with the same frequency as the string. Some of the energy is lost in the form of thermal energy transferred to the air. You may recall from the chapter on waves that areas of compression and rarefaction in longitudinal waves (such as sound) are analogous to crests and troughs in transverse waves .","The amplitude of a sound wave decreases with distance from its source, because the energy of the wave is spread over a larger and larger area. But some of the energy is also absorbed by objects, such as the eardrum in [Figure 14.1], and some of the energy is converted to thermal energy in the air. [Figure 14.1] shows a graph of gauge pressure versus distance from the vibrating string. From this figure, you can see that the compression of a longitudinal wave is analogous to the peak of a transverse wave, and the rarefaction of a longitudinal wave is analogous to the trough of a transverse wave. Just as a transverse wave alternates between peaks and troughs, a longitudinal wave alternates between compression and rarefaction."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063517281","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_StringR.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_StringR.jpg","alt":"A string bulges to the right, creating compression ahead of it and rarefaction behind it."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063699203","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_StringL.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_StringL.jpg","alt":"A string bulges to the left, creating compression ahead of it and rarefaction behind it."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067165375","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Vibrations.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Vibrations.jpg","alt":"A string vibrates back and forth from left to right. The graph of its position is sinusoidal in shape, with amplitude decreasing over time."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063629448","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Eardrum.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Eardrum.jpg","alt":"A sound wave with areas of alternating compression and rarefaction enter an ear canal and eardrum."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.sub02","title":"The Speed of Sound","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["The speed of sound varies greatly depending upon the medium it is traveling through. The speed of sound in a medium is determined by a combination of the medium’s rigidity (or compressibility in gases) and its density. The more rigid (or less compressible) the medium, the faster the speed of sound. The greater the density of a medium, the slower the speed of sound. The speed of sound in air is low, because air is compressible. Because liquids and solids are relatively rigid and very difficult to compress, the speed of sound in such media is generally greater than in gases. [Table_14_01] shows the speed of sound in various media. Since temperature affects density, the speed of sound varies with the temperature of the medium through which it’s traveling to some extent, especially for gases."],"figures":[],"tables":[{"sourceId":"Table_14_01","headers":["Medium","v w (m/s)"],"rows":[["Gases at 0 °C"],["Air","331"],["Carbon dioxide","259"],["Oxygen","316"],["Helium","965"],["Hydrogen","1290"],["Liquids at 20 °C"],["Ethanol","1160"],["Mercury","1450"],["Water, fresh","1480"],["Sea water","1540"],["Human tissue","1540"],["Solids (longitudinal or bulk)"],["Vulcanized rubber","54"],["Polyethylene","920"],["Marble","3810"],["Glass, Pyrex","5640"],["Lead","1960"],["Aluminum","5120"],["Steel","5960"]]}]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.sub03","title":"The Relationship Between the Speed of Sound and the Frequency and Wavelength of a Sound Wave","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Sound, like all waves, travels at certain speeds through different media and has the properties of frequency and wavelength . Sound travels much slower than light—you can observe this while watching a fireworks display (see [Figure 14.1]), since the flash of an explosion is seen before its sound is heard.","The relationship between the speed of sound, its frequency, and wavelength is the same as for all waves:","where v is the speed of sound (in units of m/s), f is its frequency (in units of hertz), and \\(\\lambda\\) is its wavelength (in units of meters). Recall that wavelength is defined as the distance between adjacent identical parts of a wave. The wavelength of a sound, therefore, is the distance between adjacent identical parts of a sound wave. Just as the distance between adjacent crests in a transverse wave is one wavelength, the distance between adjacent compressions in a sound wave is also one wavelength, as shown in [Figure 14.1]. The frequency of a sound wave is the same as that of the source. For example, a tuning fork vibrating at a given frequency would produce sound waves that oscillate at that same frequency. The frequency of a sound is the number of waves that pass a point per unit time.","One of the more important properties of sound is that its speed is nearly independent of frequency. If this were not the case, and high-frequency sounds traveled faster, for example, then the farther you were from a band in a football stadium, the more the sound from the low-pitch instruments would lag behind the high-pitch ones. But the music from all instruments arrives in cadence independent of distance, and so all frequencies must travel at nearly the same speed.","Recall that \\(v = f\\lambda\\), and in a given medium under fixed temperature and humidity, v is constant. Therefore, the relationship between f and \\(\\lambda\\) is inverse: The higher the frequency, the shorter the wavelength of a sound wave.","The speed of sound can change when sound travels from one medium to another. However, the frequency usually remains the same because it is like a driven oscillation and maintains the frequency of the original source. If v changes and f remains the same, then the wavelength \\(\\lambda\\) must change. Since \\(v = f\\lambda\\), the higher the speed of a sound, the greater its wavelength for a given frequency.","This simulation lets you see sound waves. Adjust the frequency or amplitude (volume) and you can see and hear how the wave changes. Move the listener around and hear what she hears. Switch to the Two Source Interference tab or the Interference by Reflection tab to experiment with interference and reflection."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067023115","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Fireworks.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Fireworks.jpg","alt":"The photograph shows fireworks exploding in the night sky."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067202805","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_TuningFork.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_TuningFork.jpg","alt":"A tuning fork vibrates back and forth, creating sound waves."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.ex01","number":"14.1.1","title":"What Are the Wavelengths of Audible Sounds?","problem":"Calculate the wavelengths of sounds at the extremes of the audible range, 20 and 20,000 Hz, in conditions where sound travels at 348.7 m/s.","problemImages":[],"strategy":"To find wavelength from frequency, we can use \\(v = f\\lambda\\) .","solution":"(1) Identify the knowns. The values for v and f are given.\n(2) Solve the relationship between speed, frequency and wavelength for \\(\\lambda\\) .\n\\(\\lambda = \\frac{v}{f}\\text{.}\\)\n(3) Enter the speed and the minimum frequency to give the maximum wavelength.\n\\({\\lambda}_{max} = \\frac{348.7\\,\\text{m/s}}{20\\,\\text{Hz}} = 17\\,\\text{m} \\approx \\text{20 m (1 sig}\\text{. figure)}\\)\n(4) Enter the speed and the maximum frequency to give the minimum wavelength.\n\\({\\lambda}_{min} = \\frac{348.7\\,\\text{m/s}}{20,000\\,\\text{Hz}} = 0.017\\,\\text{m} \\approx \\text{2 cm (1 sig}\\text{. figure)}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Because the product of f multiplied by \\(\\lambda\\) equals a constant velocity in unchanging conditions, the smaller f is, the larger \\(\\lambda\\) must be, and vice versa. Note that you can also easily rearrange the same formula to find frequency or velocity."}],"snapLabs":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.lab01","title":"Voice as a Sound Wave","text":"In this lab you will observe the effects of blowing and speaking into a piece of paper in order to compare and contrast different sound waves.\nsheet of paper\ntape\ntable\nInstructions\nProcedure\nSuspend a sheet of paper so that the top edge of the paper is fixed and the bottom edge is free to move. You could tape the top edge of the paper to the edge of a table, for example.\nGently blow air near the edge of the bottom of the sheet and note how the sheet moves.\nSpeak softly and then louder such that the sounds hit the edge of the bottom of the paper, and note how the sheet moves.\nInterpret the results.","images":[]}],"tipsAndFeatures":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.feat01","type":"tip","title":"Tips for Success","text":"Make sure to have audio enabled and set to Listener rather than Speaker, or else the sound will not vary as you move the listener around.","images":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.feat02","type":"physics-in-action","title":"Echolocation","text":"A bat uses sound echoes to find its way about and to catch prey. The time for the echo to return is directly proportional to the distance.\nEcholocation is the use of reflected sound waves to locate and identify objects. It is used by animals such as bats, dolphins and whales, and is also imitated by humans in SONAR—Sound Navigation and Ranging—and echolocation technology.\nBats, dolphins and whales use echolocation to navigate and find food in their environment. They locate an object (or obstacle) by emitting a sound and then sensing the reflected sound waves. Since the speed of sound in air is constant, the time it takes for the sound to travel to the object and back gives the animal a sense of the distance between itself and the object. This is called ranging . [Figure 14.1] shows a bat using echolocation to sense distances.\nEcholocating animals identify an object by comparing the relative intensity of the sound waves returning to each ear to figure out the angle at which the sound waves were reflected. This gives information about the direction, size and shape of the object. Since there is a slight distance in position between the two ears of an animal, the sound may return to one of the ears with a bit of a delay, which also provides information about the position of the object. For example, if a bear is directly to the right of a bat, the echo will return to the bat’s left ear later than to its right ear. If, however, the bear is directly ahead of the bat, the echo would return to both ears at the same time. For an animal without a sense of sight such as a bat, it is important to know where other animals are as well as what they are; their survival depends on it.\nPrinciples of echolocation have been used to develop a variety of useful sensing technologies. SONAR, is used by submarines to detect objects underwater and measure water depth. Unlike animal echolocation, which relies on only one transmitter (a mouth) and two receivers (ears), manmade SONAR uses many transmitters and beams to get a more accurate reading of the environment. Radar technologies use the echo of radio waves to locate clouds and storm systems in weather forecasting, and to locate aircraft for air traffic control. Some new cars use echolocation technology to sense obstacles around the car, and warn the driver who may be about to hit something (or even to automatically parallel park). Echolocation technologies and training systems are being developed to help visually impaired people navigate their everyday environments.","images":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067126844","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Bat.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_01_Bat.jpg","alt":"A bat produces sound waves that bounce off of a fly and back to the bat."}]}],"summaryPoints":["Sound is one type of wave.","Sound is a disturbance of matter that is transmitted from its source outward in the form of longitudinal waves.","The relationship of the speed of sound v , its frequency f , and its wavelength \\(\\lambda\\) is given by \\(v = f\\lambda\\), which is the same relationship given for all waves.","The speed of sound depends upon the medium through which the sound wave is travelling.","In a given medium at a specific temperature (or density), the speed of sound v is the same for all frequencies and wavelengths."],"glossary":[{"term":"rarefaction","termVi":"Vùng dãn (trong sóng âm)","definition":"a low-pressure region in a sound wave","definitionVi":"Vùng áp suất thấp trong sóng dọc nơi các phân tử không khí giãn ra xa nhau (xen kẽ với vùng nén Compression).","memoryTipVi":"Compression (nén) ↔ Rarefaction (dãn)."},{"term":"sound","termVi":"Sóng âm","definition":"a disturbance of matter that is transmitted from its source outward by longitudinal waves","definitionVi":"Sóng cơ học dọc lan truyền qua môi trường rắn, lỏng, khí (tai người nghe được từ 20 Hz đến 20.000 Hz).","memoryTipVi":"Âm thanh truyền nhanh nhất trong chất RẮN, chậm nhất trong chất KHÍ và KHÔNG truyền được trong chân không!"}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q002","sourceId":"fs-id1167063518963","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What sort of motion do the particles of a medium experience when a sound wave passes through it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Simple harmonic motion"},{"label":"b","text":"Circular motion"},{"label":"c","text":"Random motion"},{"label":"d","text":"Translational motion"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167067070336","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What does the speed of sound depend on?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The wavelength of the wave"},{"label":"b","text":"The size of the medium"},{"label":"c","text":"The frequency of the wave"},{"label":"d","text":"The properties of the medium"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q004","sourceId":"fs-id1167066992751","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What property of a gas would affect the speed of sound traveling through it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The volume of the gas"},{"label":"b","text":"The flammability of the gas"},{"label":"c","text":"The mass of the gas"},{"label":"d","text":"The compressibility of the gas"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q007","sourceId":"fs-id1167066875611","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the amplitude of a sound wave perceived by the human ear?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"loudness"},{"label":"b","text":"pitch"},{"label":"c","text":"intensity"},{"label":"d","text":"timbre"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q008","sourceId":"fs-id1167063788627","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"The compressibility of air and hydrogen is almost the same. Which factor is the reason that sound travels faster in hydrogen than in air?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Hydrogen is more dense than air."},{"label":"b","text":"Hydrogen is less dense than air."},{"label":"c","text":"Hydrogen atoms are heavier than air molecules."},{"label":"d","text":"Hydrogen atoms are lighter than air molecules."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q015","sourceId":"fs-id1167061315126","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What properties does a loud, shrill whistle have?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"high amplitude, high frequency"},{"label":"b","text":"high amplitude, low frequency"},{"label":"c","text":"low amplitude, high frequency"},{"label":"d","text":"low amplitude, low frequency"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q020","sourceId":"fs-id1167061190377","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What component of a longitudinal sound wave is analogous to a trough of a transverse wave?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"compression"},{"label":"b","text":"rarefaction"},{"label":"c","text":"node"},{"label":"d","text":"antinode"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q021","sourceId":"fs-id1167067045688","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the frequency of a sound wave as perceived by the human ear?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"timbre"},{"label":"b","text":"loudness"},{"label":"c","text":"intensity"},{"label":"d","text":"pitch"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q022","sourceId":"fs-id1167061706252","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What properties of a solid determine the speed of sound traveling through it?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"mass and density"},{"label":"b","text":"rigidity and density"},{"label":"c","text":"volume and density"},{"label":"d","text":"shape and rigidity"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q023","sourceId":"fs-id1167067054070","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Does the density of a medium affect the speed of sound?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"No"},{"label":"b","text":"Yes"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q024","sourceId":"fs-id1167067145274","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Does a bat make use of the properties of sound waves to locate its prey?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"No"},{"label":"b","text":"Yes"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q025","sourceId":"fs-id1167063573010","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Do the properties of a sound wave change when it travels from one medium to another?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"No"},{"label":"b","text":"Yes"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec01.q028","sourceId":"fs-id1167066946956","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Is the speed of sound dependent on its frequency?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"No"},{"label":"b","text":"Yes"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":3,"keyEquationCount":1,"subsectionCount":3,"workedExampleCount":1,"snapLabCount":1,"glossaryCount":2,"assessmentCount":13}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02","moduleId":"m54333","number":"14.2","slug":"sound-intensity-and-sound-level","title":"Sound Intensity and Sound Level","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Relate amplitude of a wave to loudness and energy of a sound wave","Describe the decibel scale for measuring sound intensity","Solve problems involving the intensity of a sound wave","Describe how humans produce and hear sounds"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Độ cao (Pitch ↔ Tần số f), Độ to (Loudness ↔ Biên độ/Cường độ I) và thang đo lôgarit đêxiben (dB).","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"+10 dB increase","meaningVi":"Tăng 10 dB → Cường độ âm I gấp 10 lần","mathNotation":"×10 intensity"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Tăng 20 dB nghĩa là cường độ âm tăng 10² = 100 lần (KHÔNG phải 20 lần!)."]},"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.sub01","title":"Amplitude, Loudness and Energy of a Sound Wave","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["In a quiet forest, you can sometimes hear a single leaf fall to the ground. But in a traffic jam filled with honking cars, you may have to shout just so the person next to you can hear [Figure 14.2].The loudness of a sound is related to how energetically its source is vibrating. In cartoons showing a screaming person, the cartoonist often shows an open mouth with a vibrating uvula (the hanging tissue at the back of the mouth) to represent a loud sound coming from the throat. [Figure 14.2] shows such a cartoon depiction of a bird loudly expressing its opinion.","A useful quantity for describing the loudness of sounds is called sound intensity . In general, the intensity of a wave is the power per unit area carried by the wave. Power is the rate at which energy is transferred by the wave. In equation form, intensity I is","where P is the power through an area A . The SI unit for I is W/m 2 . The intensity of a sound depends upon its pressure amplitude. The relationship between the intensity of a sound wave and its pressure amplitude (or pressure variation Δ p ) is","where ρ is the density of the material in which the sound wave travels, in units of kg/m 3 , and v is the speed of sound in the medium, in units of m/s. Pressure amplitude has units of pascals (Pa) or N/m 2 . Note that Δ p is half the difference between the maximum and minimum pressure in the sound wave.","We can see from the equation that the intensity of a sound is proportional to its amplitude squared. The pressure variation is proportional to the amplitude of the oscillation, and so I varies as (Δ p ) 2 . This relationship is consistent with the fact that the sound wave is produced by some vibration; the greater its pressure amplitude, the more the air is compressed during the vibration. Because the power of a sound wave is the rate at which energy is transferred, the energy of a sound wave is also proportional to its amplitude squared."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062325840","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Traffic.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Traffic.jpg","alt":"Multiple lanes of traffic crowd a busy street."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062966365","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Bird.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Bird.jpg","alt":"This figure has two panels. In the upper panel, a bird chirping at low volume produces sound waves with small amplitudes. In the lower panel, a bird chirping at high volume produces sound waves with large amplitudes."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.sub02","title":"The Decibel Scale","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["You may have noticed that when people talk about the loudness of a sound, they describe it in units of decibels rather than watts per meter squared. While sound intensity (in W/m 2 ) is the SI unit, the sound intensity level in decibels (dB) is more relevant for how humans perceive sounds. The way our ears perceive sound can be more accurately described by the logarithm of the intensity of a sound rather than the intensity of a sound directly. The sound intensity level β is defined to be","where I is sound intensity in watts per meter squared, and I 0 = 10 –12 W/m 2 is a reference intensity. I 0 is chosen as the reference point because it is the lowest intensity of sound a person with normal hearing can perceive. The decibel level of a sound having an intensity of 10 –12 W/m 2 is β = 0 dB, because log 10 1 = 0. That is, the threshold of human hearing is 0 decibels.","Each factor of 10 in intensity corresponds to 10 dB. For example, a 90 dB sound compared with a 60 dB sound is 30 dB greater, or three factors of 10 (that is, 10 3 times) as intense. Another example is that if one sound is 10 7 as intense as another, it is 70 dB higher.","Since β is defined in terms of a ratio, it is unit-less. The unit called deci bel (dB) is used to indicate that this ratio is multiplied by 10. The sound intensity level is not the same as sound intensity—it tells you the level of the sound relative to a reference intensity rather than the actual intensity."],"figures":[],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.sub03","title":"Hearing and Voice","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["People create sounds by pushing air up through their lungs and through elastic folds in the throat called vocal cords. These folds open and close rhythmically, creating a pressure buildup. As air travels up and past the vocal cords, it causes them to vibrate. This vibration escapes the mouth along with puffs of air as sound. A voice changes in pitch when the muscles of the larynx relax or tighten, changing the tension on the vocal chords. A voice becomes louder when air flow from the lungs increases, making the amplitude of the sound pressure wave greater.","Hearing is the perception of sound. It can give us plenty of information—such as pitch, loudness, and direction. Humans can normally hear frequencies ranging from approximately 20 to 20,000 Hz. Other animals have hearing ranges different from that of humans. Dogs can hear sounds as high as 45,000 Hz, whereas bats and dolphins can hear up to 110,000 Hz sounds. You may have noticed that dogs respond to the sound of a dog whistle which produces sound out of the range of human hearing.","Sounds below 20 Hz are called infrasound , whereas those above 20,000 Hz are ultrasound . The perception of frequency is called pitch , and the perception of intensity is called loudness .","The way we hear involves some interesting physics. The sound wave that hits our ear is a pressure wave. The ear converts sound waves into electrical nerve impulses, similar to a microphone.","[Figure 14.2] shows the anatomy of the ear with its division into three parts: the outer ear or ear canal; the middle ear, which runs from the eardrum to the cochlea; and the inner ear, which is the cochlea itself. The body part normally referred to as the ear is technically called the pinna.","The outer ear, or ear canal, carries sound to the eardrum protected inside of the ear. The middle ear converts sound into mechanical vibrations and applies these vibrations to the cochlea. The lever system of the middle ear takes the force exerted on the eardrum by sound pressure variations, amplifies it and transmits it to the inner ear via the oval window. Two muscles in the middle ear protect the inner ear from very intense sounds. They react to intense sound in a few milliseconds and reduce the force transmitted to the cochlea. This protective reaction can also be triggered by your own voice, so that humming during a fireworks display, for example, can reduce noise damage.","[Figure 14.2] shows the middle and inner ear in greater detail. As the middle ear bones vibrate, they vibrate the cochlea, which contains fluid. This creates pressure waves in the fluid that cause the tectorial membrane to vibrate. The motion of the tectorial membrane stimulates tiny cilia on specialized cells called hair cells. These hair cells, and their attached neurons, transform the motion of the tectorial membrane into electrical signals that are sent to the brain.","The tectorial membrane vibrates at different positions based on the frequency of the incoming sound. This allows us to detect the pitch of sound. Additional processing in the brain also allows us to determine which direction the sound is coming from (based on comparison of the sound’s arrival time and intensity between our two ears).","Yet another way that people make sounds is through playing musical instruments (see the previous figure). Recall that the perception of frequency is called pitch. You may have noticed that the pitch range produced by an instrument tends to depend upon its size. Small instruments, such as a piccolo, typically make high-pitch sounds, while larger instruments, such as a tuba, typically make low-pitch sounds. High-pitch means small wavelength, and the size of a musical instrument is directly related to the wavelengths of sound it produces. So a small instrument creates short-wavelength sounds, just as a large instrument creates long-wavelength sounds.","Most of us have excellent relative pitch, which means that we can tell whether one sound has a different frequency from another. We can usually distinguish one sound from another if the frequencies of the two sounds differ by as little as 1 Hz. For example, 500.0 and 501.5 Hz are noticeably different.","Musical notes are particular sounds that can be produced by most instruments, and are the building blocks of a song. In Western music, musical notes have particular names, such as A-sharp, C, or E-flat. Some people can identify musical notes just by listening to them. This rare ability is called perfect , or absolute, pitch .","When a violin plays middle C, there is no mistaking it for a piano playing the same note. The reason is that each instrument produces a distinctive set of frequencies and intensities. We call our perception of these combinations of frequencies and intensities the timbre of the sound. It is more difficult to quantify timbre than loudness or pitch. Timbre is more subjective. Evocative adjectives such as dull, brilliant, warm, cold, pure, and rich are used to describe the timbre of a sound rather than quantities with units, which makes for a difficult topic to dissect with physics. So the consideration of timbre takes us into the realm of perceptual psychology, where higher-level processes in the brain are dominant. This is also true for other perceptions of sound, such as music and noise. But as a teenager, you are likely already aware that one person’s music may be another person’s noise."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065770299","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Ear.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Ear.jpg","alt":"The outer ear contains the ear canal, the middle ear contains the eardrum and oval window, and the inner ear contains the cochlea."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062811456","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Ear2.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Ear2.jpg","alt":"The cochlea is a coiled tube with cilia inside, located in the inner ear."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167063167987","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Guitar.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_02_Guitar.jpg","alt":"A man plays the guitar while his friends sing along around a campfire."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.sub04","title":"References","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Nave, R. Vocal sound production—HyperPhysics. Retrieved from http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/music/voice.html"],"figures":[],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.ex01","number":"14.2.1","title":"Calculating Sound Intensity Levels: Sound Waves","problem":"Calculate the sound intensity level in decibels for a sound wave traveling in air at 0 ºC and having a pressure amplitude of 0.656 Pa.","problemImages":[],"strategy":"We are given Δ p , so we can calculate I using the equation \\(I = \\frac{{(\\Delta p)}^{2}}{2\\rho {v}_{}}\\) . Using I , we can calculate β straight from its definition in \\(\\beta \\text{(}d\\text{B)} = 10{log}_{10}(\\frac{I}{{I}_{0}})\\)\n\\(\\beta \\text{(dB)} = 10{\\text{log}}_{10}(\\frac{I}{{I}_{0}})\\) .","solution":"(1) Identify knowns:\nSound travels at 331 m/s in air at 0 °C.\nAir has a density of 1.29 kg/m 3 at atmospheric pressure and 0ºC.\n(2) Enter these values and the pressure amplitude into \\(I = \\frac{{(\\Delta p)}^{2}}{2\\rho {v}_{w}}\\) .\n\\(I = \\frac{{(\\Delta p)}^{2}}{2\\rho {v}_{w}} = \\frac{{(0.656\\,\\text{Pa})}^{2}}{2(1.29{\\text{kg/m}}^{3})(331\\,\\text{m/s})} = 5.04 \\times {10}^{- 4}{\\text{W/m}}^{2}\\text{.}\\)\n(3) Enter the value for I and the known value for I 0 into \\(\\beta \\text{(dB)} = 10{\\text{log}}_{10}(\\frac{I}{{I}_{0}})\\) . Calculate to find the sound intensity level in decibels.\n\\(10{\\text{log}}_{10}(5.04 \\times {10}^{8}) = 10(8.70)\\text{dB} = 87.0\\,\\text{dB.}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"This 87.0 dB sound has an intensity five times as great as an 80 dB sound. So a factor of five in intensity corresponds to a difference of 7 dB in sound intensity level. This value is true for any intensities differing by a factor of five."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.ex02","number":"14.2.2","title":"Change Intensity Levels of a Sound: What Happens to the Decibel Level?","problem":"Show that if one sound is twice as intense as another, it has a sound level about 3 dB higher.","problemImages":[],"strategy":"You are given that the ratio of two intensities is 2 to 1, and are then asked to find the difference in their sound levels in decibels. You can solve this problem using of the properties of logarithms.","solution":"(1) Identify knowns:\nThe ratio of the two intensities is 2 to 1, or: \\(\\frac{{I}_{2}}{{I}_{1}} = 2.00\\,\\text{.}\\)\nWe want to show that the difference in sound levels is about 3 dB. That is, we want to show\n\\({\\beta}_{2} - {\\beta}_{1} = 3\\,\\text{dB.}\\)\nNote that\n\\({log}_{10}b - {log}_{10}a{\\text{= log}}_{10}(\\frac{b}{a})\\text{.}\\)\n(2) Use the definition of β to get\n\\({\\beta}_{2} - {\\beta}_{1} = 10{\\text{log}}_{10}(\\frac{{I}_{2}}{{I}_{1}}) = 10{\\text{log}}_{10}2.00 = 10(0.301)\\text{dB.}\\)\nTherefore, \\({\\beta}_{2} - {\\beta}_{1} = 3.01\\,\\text{dB.}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"This means that the two sound intensity levels differ by 3.01 dB, or about 3 dB, as advertised. Note that because only the ratio I 2 / I 1 is given (and not the actual intensities), this result is true for any intensities that differ by a factor of two. For example, a 56.0 dB sound is twice as intense as a 53.0 dB sound, a 97.0 dB sound is half as intense as a 100 dB sound, and so on."}],"snapLabs":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.lab01","title":"Feeling Sound","text":"In this lab, you will play music with a heavy beat to literally feel the vibrations and explore what happens when the volume changes.\nCD player or portable electronic device connected to speakers\nrock or rap music CD or mp3\na lightweight table\nProcedure\nPlace the speakers on a light table, and start playing the CD or mp3.\nPlace your hand gently on the table next to the speakers.\nIncrease the volume and note the level when the table just begins to vibrate as the music plays.\nIncrease the reading on the volume control until it doubles. What has happened to the vibrations?","images":[]}],"tipsAndFeatures":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.feat01","type":"tip","title":"Tips for Success","text":"Pressure is usually denoted by capital P , but we are using a lowercase p for pressure in this case to distinguish it from power P above.","images":[]}],"summaryPoints":["The intensity of a sound is proportional to its amplitude squared.","The energy of a sound wave is also proportional to its amplitude squared.","Sound intensity level in decibels (dB) is more relevant for how humans perceive sounds than sound intensity (in W/m 2 ), even though sound intensity is the SI unit.","Sound intensity level is not the same as sound intensity—it tells you the level of the sound relative to a reference intensity rather than the actual intensity.","Hearing is the perception of sound and involves that transformation of sound waves into vibrations of parts within the ear. These vibrations are then transformed into neural signals that are interpreted by the brain.","People create sounds by pushing air up through their lungs and through elastic folds in the throat called vocal cords."],"glossary":[{"term":"amplitude","termVi":"Biên độ sóng âm","definition":"the amount that matter is disrupted during a sound wave, as measured by the difference in height between the crests and troughs of the sound wave.","definitionVi":"Độ lệch cực đại của áp suất/phần tử — quyết định độ to của âm.","memoryTipVi":"Biên độ càng lớn → Âm càng TO."},{"term":"decibel","termVi":"Đêxiben (dB)","definition":"a unit used to describe sound intensity levels","definitionVi":"Đơn vị đo mức cường độ âm (ngưỡng nghe 0 dB, ngưỡng đau ~120–130 dB).","memoryTipVi":"1 Bel = 10 decibels."},{"term":"hearing","termVi":"Thính giác (Khả năng nghe)","definition":"the perception of sound","definitionVi":"Cảm nhận của tai người đối với sóng âm trong dải tần 20 Hz – 20 kHz.","memoryTipVi":"Dưới 20 Hz là Hạ âm (Infrasound), trên 20 kHz là Siêu âm (Ultrasound)."},{"term":"loudness","termVi":"Độ to của âm","definition":"the perception of sound intensity","definitionVi":"Cảm giác sinh lý về độ mạnh của âm (phụ thuộc chủ yếu vào mức cường độ âm và tần số).","memoryTipVi":"Loudness (độ to) ↔ Amplitude/Intensity."},{"term":"pitch","termVi":"Độ cao của âm","definition":"the perception of the frequency of a sound","definitionVi":"Cảm giác âm trầm hay bổng — quyết định bởi TẦN SỐ (Frequency) của sóng âm.","memoryTipVi":"Pitch (độ cao) ↔ Frequency (tần số)."},{"term":"sound intensity","termVi":"Cường độ âm (I)","definition":"the power per unit area carried by a sound wave","definitionVi":"Công suất sóng âm truyền qua một đơn vị diện tích vuông góc: I = P / A (đơn vị W/m²).","memoryTipVi":"Cường độ âm tỉ lệ thuận với bình phương biên độ."},{"term":"sound intensity level","termVi":"Mức cường độ âm (β)","definition":"the level of sound relative to a fixed standard related to human hearing","definitionVi":"Thang đo lôgarit cường độ âm tính theo đơn vị đêxiben (dB): β = 10 log(I / I₀).","memoryTipVi":"Tăng thêm 10 dB tương ứng với cường độ âm I tăng gấp 10 lần!"}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167066088746","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What word or phrase is used to describe the loudness of sound?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"frequency or oscillation"},{"label":"b","text":"intensity level or decibel"},{"label":"c","text":"timbre"},{"label":"d","text":"pitch"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q006","sourceId":"fs-id1167065892043","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"How do humans change the pitch of their voice?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Relaxing or tightening their glottis"},{"label":"b","text":"Relaxing or tightening their uvula"},{"label":"c","text":"Relaxing or tightening their tongue"},{"label":"d","text":"Relaxing or tightening their larynx"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q007","sourceId":"fs-id1167064990876","category":"practice-problems","categoryLabel":"Practice Problems (Bài tập vận dụng)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Calculate the intensity of a wave if the power transferred is 10 W and the area through which the wave is transferred is 5 square meters.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"200 W / m 2"},{"label":"b","text":"50 W / m 2"},{"label":"c","text":"0.5 W / m 2"},{"label":"d","text":"2 W / m 2"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q009","sourceId":"fs-id1167066045522","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the mathematical relationship between intensity, power, and area?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"\\(I = \\frac{P}{{A}^{2}}\\)"},{"label":"b","text":"\\(I = PA\\)"},{"label":"c","text":"\\(I = \\frac{A}{P}\\)"},{"label":"d","text":"\\(I = \\frac{P}{A}\\)"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q010","sourceId":"fs-id1167062352586","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"How does the \"decibel\" get its name?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The meaning of deci is hundred and the number of decibels is one-hundredth of the logarithm to base 10 of the ratio of two sound intensities."},{"label":"b","text":"The meaning of deci is \"ten\" and the number of decibels is one-tenth of the logarithm to base 10 of the ratio of two sound intensities."},{"label":"c","text":"The meaning of deci is one-hundredth and the number of decibels is hundred times the logarithm to base 10 of the ratio of two sound intensities."},{"label":"d","text":"The meaning of deci is one-tenth and the number of decibels is ten times the logarithm to base 10 of the ratio of two sound intensities."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q016","sourceId":"fs-id1167064990876","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Calculate the intensity of a wave if the power transferred is 10 W and the area through which the wave is transferred is 5 square meters.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"200 W / m 2"},{"label":"b","text":"50 W / m 2"},{"label":"c","text":"0.5 W / m 2"},{"label":"d","text":"2 W / m 2"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q018","sourceId":"fs-id1167064985796","category":"problems","categoryLabel":"Problems (Bài toán tính toán)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Calculate the sound intensity for a sound wave traveling through air at 15° C and having a pressure amplitude of 0.80 Pa. (Hint—Speed of sound in air at 15° C is 340 m/s .)","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"9.6×10 -3 W / m 2"},{"label":"b","text":"7.7×10 -3 W / m 2"},{"label":"c","text":"9.6×10 -4 W / m 2"},{"label":"d","text":"7.7×10 -4 W / m 2"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q020","sourceId":"fs-id1167065039534","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Which of the following terms is a useful quantity to describe the loudness of a sound?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"intensity"},{"label":"b","text":"frequency"},{"label":"c","text":"pitch"},{"label":"d","text":"wavelength"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q021","sourceId":"fs-id1167064597655","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the unit of sound intensity level?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"decibels"},{"label":"b","text":"hertz"},{"label":"c","text":"watts"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q024","sourceId":"eip-id1166847264287","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Why is I 0 chosen as the reference for sound intensity?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Because, it is the highest intensity of sound a person with normal hearing can perceive at a frequency of 100 Hz."},{"label":"b","text":"Because, it is the lowest intensity of sound a person with normal hearing can perceive at a frequency of 100 Hz."},{"label":"c","text":"Because, it is the highest intensity of sound a person with normal hearing can perceive at a frequency of 1000 Hz."},{"label":"d","text":"Because, it is the lowest intensity of sound a person with normal hearing can perceive at a frequency of 1000 Hz."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec02.q031","sourceId":"fs-id1167064710364","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Which nerve carries auditory information to the brain?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"buccal nerve"},{"label":"b","text":"peroneal nerve"},{"label":"c","text":"cochlear nerve"},{"label":"d","text":"mandibular nerve"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":4,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":4,"workedExampleCount":2,"snapLabCount":1,"glossaryCount":7,"assessmentCount":11}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03","moduleId":"m54334","number":"14.3","slug":"doppler-effect-and-sonic-booms","title":"Doppler Effect and Sonic Booms","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Describe the Doppler effect of sound waves","Explain a sonic boom","Calculate the frequency shift of sound from a moving object by the Doppler shift formula, and calculate the speed of an object by the Doppler shift formula"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Hiệu ứng Doppler (tần số nghe được tăng khi lại gần, giảm khi ra xa) và Tiếng nổ siêu thanh (Sonic Boom).","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"approaching / receding source","meaningVi":"Nguồn âm lại gần (f tăng) / đi ra xa (f giảm)","mathNotation":"Doppler"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Hiệu ứng Doppler làm thay đổi tần số NGHE ĐƯỢC, chứ tần số thật của còi xe không hề thay đổi!"]},"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.sub01","title":"The Doppler Effect of Sound Waves","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["The Doppler effect is a change in the observed pitch of a sound, due to relative motion between the source and the observer. An example of the Doppler effect due to the motion of a source occurs when you are standing still, and the sound of a siren coming from an ambulance shifts from high-pitch to low-pitch as it passes by. The closer the ambulance is to you, the more sudden the shift. The faster the ambulance moves, the greater the shift. We also hear this shift in frequency for passing race cars, airplanes, and trains. An example of the Doppler effect with a stationary source and moving observer is if you ride a train past a stationary warning bell, you will hear the bell’s frequency shift from high to low as you pass by.","What causes the Doppler effect? Let’s compare three different scenarios: Sound waves emitted by a stationary source ([Figure 14.3]), sound waves emitted by a moving source ([Figure 14.3]), and sound waves emitted by a stationary source but heard by moving observers ([Figure 14.3]). In each case, the sound spreads out from the point where it was emitted.","If the source and observers are stationary, then observers on either side see the same wavelength and frequency as emitted by the source. But if the source is moving and continues to emit sound as it travels, then the air compressions (crests) become closer together in the direction in which it’s traveling and farther apart in the direction it’s traveling away from. Therefore, the wavelength is shorter in the direction the source is moving (on the right in [Figure 14.3]), and longer in the opposite direction (on the left in [Figure 14.3]).","Finally, if the observers move, as in [Figure 14.3], the frequency at which they receive the compressions changes. The observer moving toward the source receives them at a higher frequency (and therefore shorter wavelength), and the person moving away from the source receives them at a lower frequency (and therefore longer wavelength).","We know that wavelength and frequency are related by \\(v = f\\lambda \\text{,}\\) where v is the fixed speed of sound. The sound moves in a medium and has the same speed v in that medium whether the source is moving or not. Therefore, f multiplied by \\(\\lambda\\) is a constant. Because the observer on the right in [Figure 14.3] receives a shorter wavelength, the frequency she perceives must be higher. Similarly, the observer on the left receives a longer wavelength and therefore perceives a lower frequency.","The same thing happens in [Figure 14.3]. A higher frequency is perceived by the observer moving toward the source, and a lower frequency is perceived by an observer moving away from the source. In general, then, relative motion of source and observer toward one another increases the perceived frequency. Relative motion apart decreases the perceived frequency. The greater the relative speed is, the greater the effect.","For a stationary observer and a moving source of sound, the frequency ( f obs ) of sound perceived by the observer is","where f s is the frequency of sound from a source, v s is the speed of the source along a line joining the source and observer, and v w is the speed of sound. The minus sign is used for motion toward the observer and the plus sign for motion away from the observer.","Note that the greater the speed of the source, the greater the Doppler effect. Similarly, for a stationary source and moving observer, the frequency perceived by the observer f obs is given by","where v obs is the speed of the observer along a line joining the source and observer. Here the plus sign is for motion toward the source, and the minus sign is for motion away from the source."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067199495","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_Stationary.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_Stationary.jpg","alt":"Sounds emitted by a stationary car spread out in spherical waves as stationary observers listen on both sides."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067074217","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_MovingSrc.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_MovingSrc.jpg","alt":"Sound waves are compressed ahead of the motion of a car and stretched out in the wake of the motion of the car as stationary observers listen on both sides."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067051451","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_MovingObs.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_MovingObs.jpg","alt":"One observer runs away from the sound source (a car) while another runs toward it."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.sub02","title":"Sonic Booms","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["What happens to the sound produced by a moving source, such as a jet airplane, that approaches or even exceeds the speed of sound? Suppose a jet airplane is coming nearly straight at you, emitting a sound of frequency f s . The greater the plane’s speed, v s , the greater the Doppler shift and the greater the value of f obs . Now, as v s approaches the speed of sound, v w , f obs approaches infinity, because the denominator in \\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} - {v}_{s}})\\) approaches zero.","This result means that at the speed of sound, in front of the source, each wave is superimposed on the previous one because the source moves forward at the speed of sound. The observer gets them all at the same instant, and so the frequency is theoretically infinite. If the source exceeds the speed of sound, no sound is received by the observer until the source has passed, so that the sounds from the source when it was approaching are stacked up with those from it when receding, creating a sonic boom . A sonic boom is a constructive interference of sound created by an object moving faster than sound.","An aircraft creates two sonic booms, one from its nose and one from its tail (see [Figure 14.3]). During television coverage of space shuttle landings, two distinct booms could often be heard. These were separated by exactly the time it would take the shuttle to pass by a point. Observers on the ground often do not observe the aircraft creating the sonic boom, because it has passed by before the shock wave reaches them. If the aircraft flies close by at low altitude, pressures in the sonic boom can be destructive enough to break windows. Because of this, supersonic flights are banned over populated areas of the United States."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167067276496","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_SonicBoom.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_03_SonicBoom.jpg","alt":"The first sonic boom trails behind the nose of the aircraft and the second trails behind the tail."}],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.ex01","number":"14.3.1","title":"Calculate Doppler Shift: A Train Horn","problem":"Suppose a train that has a 150 Hz horn is moving at 35 m/s in still air on a day when the speed of sound is 340 m/s. What frequencies are observed by a stationary person at the side of the tracks as the train approaches and after it passes?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"To find the observed frequency, \\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} \\pm {v}_{s}})\\) must be used because the source is moving. The minus sign is used for the approaching train, and the plus sign for the receding train.","solution":"(1) Enter known values into \\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} - {v}_{s}})\\) to calculate the frequency observed by a stationary person as the train approaches:\n\\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} - {v}_{s}}) = (150\\,\\text{Hz})(\\frac{340\\,\\text{m/s}}{340\\,\\text{m/s} - 35\\,\\text{m/s}})\\) \\(= 167\\,\\text{Hz} \\approx 170\\,\\text{Hz (2 sig. figs.)}\\)\n(2) Use the same equation but with the plus sign to find the frequency heard by a stationary person as the train recedes.\n\\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} + {v}_{s}}) = (150\\,\\text{Hz})(\\frac{340\\,\\text{m/s}}{340\\,\\text{m/s} + 35\\,\\text{m/s}})\\) \\(= 136\\,\\text{Hz} \\approx \\text{140 Hz (2 sig. figs.)}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"The numbers calculated are valid when the train is far enough away that the motion is nearly along the line joining the train and the observer. In both cases, the shift is significant and easily noticed. Note that the shift is approximately 20 Hz for motion toward and approximately 10 Hz for motion away. The shifts are not symmetric."}],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.feat01","type":"tip","title":"Tips for Success","text":"Rather than just memorizing rules, which are easy to forget, it is better to think about the rules of an equation intuitively. Using a minus sign in \\({f}_{obs} = {f}_{s}(\\frac{{v}_{w}}{{v}_{w} \\pm {v}_{s}})\\) will decrease the denominator and increase the observed frequency, which is consistent with the expected outcome of the Doppler effect when the source is moving toward the observer. Using a plus sign will increase the denominator and decrease the observed frequency, consistent with what you would expect for the source moving away from the observer. This may be more helpful to keep in mind rather than memorizing the fact that “the minus sign is used for motion toward the observer and the plus sign for motion away from the observer.”","images":[]}],"summaryPoints":["The Doppler effect is a shift in the observed frequency of a sound due to motion of either the source or the observer.","The observed frequency is greater than the actual source’s frequency when the source and the observer are moving closer together, either by the source moving toward the observer or the observer moving toward the source.","A sonic boom is constructive interference of sound created by an object moving faster than sound."],"glossary":[{"term":"Doppler effect","termVi":"Hiệu ứng Doppler","definition":"an alteration in the observed frequency of a sound due to relative motion between the source and the observer","definitionVi":"Sự thay đổi tần số âm nghe được khi nguồn âm hoặc người nghe chuyển động tương đối với nhau.","memoryTipVi":"Xe cứu thương lao LẠI GẦN → nghe tiếng còi CAO (tần số tăng); đi RA XA → nghe TRẦM (tần số giảm)."},{"term":"sonic boom","termVi":"Tiếng nổ siêu thanh","definition":"a constructive interference of sound created by an object moving faster than sound","definitionVi":"Sóng xung kích tạo tiếng nổ lớn khi máy bay vượt quá tốc độ âm thanh (Mach > 1).","memoryTipVi":"Tốc độ âm thanh trong không khí ~343 m/s (ở 20 °C)."}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q002","sourceId":"fs-id1167067123827","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Give an example of the Doppler effect caused by motion of the source.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The sound of a vehicle horn shifts from low-pitch to high-pitch as we move towards it."},{"label":"b","text":"The sound of a vehicle horn shifts from low-pitch to high-pitch as we move away from it."},{"label":"c","text":"The sound of a vehicle horn shifts from low-pitch to high-pitch as it passes by."},{"label":"d","text":"The sound of a vehicle horn shifts from high-pitch to low-pitch as it passes by."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q007","sourceId":"fs-id1167067279953","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Two sources of sound producing the same frequency are moving towards you at different speeds. Which one would sound more high-pitched?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"the one moving slower"},{"label":"b","text":"the one moving faster"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q010","sourceId":"fs-id1167063544083","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"When the source of sound is moving through the air, does the speed of sound change with respect to a stationary person standing nearby?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Yes"},{"label":"b","text":"No"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q022","sourceId":"fs-id1167061220883","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"True or false—The Doppler effect also occurs with waves other than sound waves.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"False"},{"label":"b","text":"True"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q026","sourceId":"fs-id1167063629514","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is the frequency of a stationary sound source if you hear it at 1200.0 Hz while moving towards it at a speed of 50.0 m/s? (Assume speed of sound to be 331 m/s.)","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"1410 Hz"},{"label":"b","text":"1380 Hz"},{"label":"c","text":"1020 Hz"},{"label":"d","text":"1042 Hz"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q027","sourceId":"fs-id1167067186132","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"True or false—The Doppler effect occurs only when the sound source is moving.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"False"},{"label":"b","text":"True"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q028","sourceId":"fs-id1167067025435","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"True or false—The observed frequency becomes infinite when the source is moving at the speed of sound.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"False"},{"label":"b","text":"True"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec03.q029","sourceId":"fs-id1167063443653","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"You are driving alongside a train. You hear its horn at a pitch that is lower than the actual frequency. What should you do to match the speed of the train? Why?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"In order to match the speed of the train, one would need to increase or decrease the speed of his/her car because a lower pitch means that either the train (the source) is moving away or that you (the observer) are moving away."},{"label":"b","text":"In order to match the speed of the train, one would need to drive at a constant speed because a lower pitch means that the train and the car are at the same speed."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":3,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":2,"workedExampleCount":1,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":2,"assessmentCount":8}},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04","moduleId":"m54335","number":"14.4","slug":"sound-interference-and-resonance","title":"Sound Interference and Resonance","isIntroduction":false,"learningObjectives":["Describe resonance and beats","Define fundamental frequency and harmonic series","Contrast an open-pipe and closed-pipe resonator","Solve problems involving harmonic series and beat frequency"],"bilingualGuide":{"notebookSummaryVi":"Cộng hưởng âm (Resonance), Họa âm (Harmonics) trong ống sáo hở 2 đầu / kín 1 đầu và Tần số phách (f_B = |f₁ - f₂|).","wordProblemCues":[{"phraseEn":"beat frequency","meaningVi":"Tần số phách = |f₁ - f₂|","mathNotation":"f_B = |f₁ - f₂|"}],"commonTrapsVi":["Ống kín 1 đầu chỉ có các họa âm BẬC LẺ (f₁, 3f₁, 5f₁...), không có họa âm bậc chẵn!"]},"keyEquations":[],"subsections":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.sub01","title":"Resonance and Beats","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Sit in front of a piano sometime and sing a loud brief note at it while pushing down on the sustain pedal. It will sing the same note back at you—the strings that have the same frequencies as your voice, are resonating in response to the forces from the sound waves that you sent to them. This is a good example of the fact that objects—in this case, piano strings—can be forced to oscillate but oscillate best at their natural frequency.","A driving force (such as your voice in the example) puts energy into a system at a certain frequency, which is not necessarily the same as the natural frequency of the system. Over time the energy dissipates, and the amplitude gradually reduces to zero- this is called damping . The natural frequency is the frequency at which a system would oscillate if there were no driving and no damping force. The phenomenon of driving a system with a frequency equal to its natural frequency is called resonance , and a system being driven at its natural frequency is said to resonate .","Most of us have played with toys where an object bobs up and down on an elastic band, something like the paddle ball suspended from a finger in [Figure 14.4]. At first you hold your finger steady, and the ball bounces up and down with a small amount of damping. If you move your finger up and down slowly, the ball will follow along without bouncing much on its own. As you increase the frequency at which you move your finger up and down, the ball will respond by oscillating with increasing amplitude. When you drive the ball at its natural frequency, the ball’s oscillations increase in amplitude with each oscillation for as long as you drive it. As the driving frequency gets progressively higher than the resonant or natural frequency, the amplitude of the oscillations becomes smaller, until the oscillations nearly disappear and your finger simply moves up and down with little effect on the ball.","Another example is that when you tune a radio, you adjust its resonant frequency so that it oscillates only at the desired station’s broadcast (driving) frequency. Also, a child on a swing is driven (pushed) by a parent at the swing’s natural frequency to reach the maximum amplitude (height). In all of these cases, the efficiency of energy transfer from the driving force into the oscillator is best at resonance.","All sound resonances are due to constructive and destructive interference. Only the resonant frequencies interfere constructively to form standing waves, while others interfere destructively and are absent. From the toot made by blowing over a bottle to the recognizability of a great singer’s voice, resonance and standing waves play a vital role in sound.","Interference happens to all types of waves, including sound waves. In fact, one way to support that something is a wave is to observe interference effects. [Figure 14.4] shows a set of headphones that employs a clever use of sound interference to cancel noise. To get destructive interference, a fast electronic analysis is performed, and a second sound is introduced with its maxima and minima exactly reversed from the incoming noise.","In addition to resonance, superposition of waves can also create beats. Beats are produced by the superposition of two waves with slightly different frequencies but the same amplitude. The waves alternate in time between constructive interference and destructive interference, giving the resultant wave an amplitude that varies over time. (See the resultant wave in [Figure 14.4]).","This wave fluctuates in amplitude, or beats, with a frequency called the beat frequency . The equation for beat frequency is","where f 1 and f 2 are the frequencies of the two original waves. If the two frequencies of sound waves are similar, then what we hear is an average frequency that gets louder and softer at the beat frequency.","For this activity , switch to the Sound tab. Turn on the Sound option, and experiment with changing the frequency and amplitude, and adding in a second speaker and a barrier."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065049539","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Finger.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Finger.jpg","alt":"A ball bobs up and down with the greatest amplitude when the finger drives its oscillation at the fundamental frequency."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066035481","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Cancel.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Cancel.jpg","alt":"Shown is a pair of adjustable, black, over-the-ear headphones."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167062458485","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Beats.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Beats.jpg","alt":"Wave 1 and wave 2 have different frequencies, and produce a resultant wave with varying amplitude."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.sub02","title":"Fundamental Frequency and Harmonics","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Suppose we hold a tuning fork near the end of a tube that is closed at the other end, as shown in [Figure 14.4], [Figure 14.4], and [Figure 14.4]. If the tuning fork has just the right frequency, the air column in the tube resonates loudly, but at most frequencies it vibrates very little. This means that the air column has only certain natural frequencies. The figures show how a resonance at the lowest of these natural frequencies is formed. A disturbance travels down the tube at the speed of sound and bounces off the closed end. If the tube is just the right length, the reflected sound arrives back at the tuning fork exactly half a cycle later, and it interferes constructively with the continuing sound produced by the tuning fork. The incoming and reflected sounds form a standing wave in the tube as shown.","The standing wave formed in the tube has its maximum air displacement (an antinode ) at the open end, and no displacement (a node ) at the closed end. Recall from the last chapter on waves that motion is unconstrained at the antinode, and halted at the node. The distance from a node to an antinode is one-fourth of a wavelength, and this equals the length of the tube; therefore, \\(\\lambda = 4L\\) . This same resonance can be produced by a vibration introduced at or near the closed end of the tube, as shown in [Figure 14.4].","Since maximum air displacements are possible at the open end and none at the closed end, there are other, shorter wavelengths that can resonate in the tube see [Figure 14.4]). Here the standing wave has three-fourths of its wavelength in the tube, or \\(L = (3/4)\\lambda ′\\), so that \\(\\lambda ′ = 4L/3\\) . There is a whole series of shorter-wavelength and higher-frequency sounds that resonate in the tube.","We use specific terms for the resonances in any system. The lowest resonant frequency is called the fundamental , while all higher resonant frequencies are called overtones . All resonant frequencies are multiples of the fundamental, and are called harmonics . The fundamental is the first harmonic, the first overtone is the second harmonic, and so on. [Figure 14.4] shows the fundamental and the first three overtones (the first four harmonics) in a tube closed at one end.","The fundamental and overtones can be present at the same time in a variety of combinations. For example, the note middle C on a trumpet sounds very different from middle C on a clarinet, even though both instruments are basically modified versions of a tube closed at one end. The fundamental frequency is the same (and usually the most intense), but the overtones and their mix of intensities are different. This mix is what gives musical instruments (and human voices) their distinctive characteristics, whether they have air columns, strings, or drumheads. In fact, much of our speech is determined by shaping the cavity formed by the throat and mouth and positioning the tongue to adjust the fundamental and combination of overtones."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065053092","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance.jpg","alt":"The vibration from a tuning fork sends a wave disturbance through the air down the tube."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065877772","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance1.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance1.jpg","alt":"The vibration from a tuning fork reflects back off the closed end of the tube."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167064875173","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance2.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_Resonance2.jpg","alt":"The disturbance is at maximum displacement as it reaches the opening of the tube."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065013854","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_ClosedEnd.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_ClosedEnd.jpg","alt":"When there is one node at the closed end, and one antinode at the open end, this forms one quarter of the complete wave cycle (and therefore, one quarter of the wavelength)."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167065858906","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_FirstOvert.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_FirstOvert.jpg","alt":"A closed pipe resonator with ¾ wavelength is shown. It has two nodes and two antinodes inside of the tube."},{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066190587","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_ClosedOver.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_ClosedOver.jpg","alt":"The fundamental has a wavelength of 4L, and a frequency of v/4L. The first overtone has a wavelength of 4L/3 and a frequency of 3v/4L. The second overtone has a wavelength of 4L/5 and a frequency of 5v/4L. The third overtone has a wavelength of 4L/7 and a frequency of 7v/4L."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.sub03","title":"Open-Pipe and Closed-Pipe Resonators","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["The resonant frequencies of a tube closed at one end (known as a closed-pipe resonator ) are \\({f}_{n} = (2n–1)\\frac{v}{4L},n = 1,2,3...,\\)","where f 1 is the fundamental, f 3 is the first overtone, and so on. Note that the resonant frequencies depend on the speed of sound v and on the length of the tube L .","Another type of tube is one that is open at both ends (known as an open-pipe resonator ). Examples are some organ pipes, flutes, and oboes. The air columns in tubes open at both ends have maximum air displacements at both ends. (See [Figure 14.4]). Standing waves form as shown.","The resonant frequencies of an open-pipe resonator are","\\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{v}{2L},n = 1,2,3...,\\)","where f 1 is the fundamental, f 2 is the first overtone, f 3 is the second overtone, and so on. Note that a tube open at both ends has a fundamental frequency twice what it would have if closed at one end. It also has a different spectrum of overtones than a tube closed at one end. So if you had two tubes with the same fundamental frequency but one was open at both ends and the other was closed at one end, they would sound different when played because they have different overtones.","Middle C, for example, would sound richer played on an open tube since it has more overtones. An open-pipe resonator has more overtones than a closed-pipe resonator because it has even multiples of the fundamental as well as odd, whereas a closed tube has only odd multiples.","In this section we have covered resonance and standing waves for wind instruments, but vibrating strings on stringed instruments also resonate and have fundamentals and overtones similar to those for wind instruments."],"figures":[{"sourceId":"fs-id1167066045989","filename":"CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_OpenOver.jpg","relativePath":"media/CNX_HSPhysics_14_04_OpenOver.jpg","alt":"The fundamental frequency is v/2L and has a wavelength of 2L. The first overtone has a wavelength of L and a frequency of v/L. The second overtone has a wavelength of 2L/3 and a frequency of 3v/2L. The third overtone has a wavelength of ½L and a frequency of 2v/L."}],"tables":[]},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.sub04","title":"Performance Task","summaryVi":null,"paragraphs":["Design and make an open air resonator capable of playing at least three different pitches (frequencies) of sound using a selection of bamboo of varying widths and lengths, which can be obtained at a local hardware store. Choose a piece of bamboo for creating a musical pipe. Calculate the length required for a certain frequency to resonate and then mark the locations where holes should be placed in the pipe to achieve their desired pitches. Use a simple hand drill or ask your wood shop department for help drilling holes. Use tuning forks to test and calibrate your instrument. Demonstrate your pipe for the class."],"figures":[],"tables":[]}],"workedExamples":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.ex01","number":"14.4.1","title":"Finding the Length of a Tube for a Closed-Pipe Resonator","problem":"If sound travels through the air at a speed of 344 m/s, what should be the length of a tube closed at one end to have a fundamental frequency of 128 Hz?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"The length L can be found by rearranging the equation \\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{v}{4L}\\)\n.","solution":"(1) Identify knowns.\nThe fundamental frequency is 128 Hz.\nThe speed of sound is 344 m/s.\n(2) Use \\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{{v}_{w}}{4L}\\) to find the fundamental frequency ( n = 1).\n\\({f}_{1} = \\frac{v}{4L}\\)\n(3) Solve this equation for length.\n\\(L = \\frac{v}{4{f}_{1}}\\)\n(4) Enter the values of the speed of sound and frequency into the expression for L .\n\\(L = \\frac{v}{4{f}_{1}} = \\frac{344\\,\\text{m/s}}{4(128\\,\\text{Hz})} = 0.672\\,\\text{m}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"Many wind instruments are modified tubes that have finger holes, valves, and other devices for changing the length of the resonating air column and therefore, the frequency of the note played. Horns producing very low frequencies, such as tubas, require tubes so long that they are coiled into loops."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.ex02","number":"14.4.2","title":"Finding the Third Overtone in an Open-Pipe Resonator","problem":"If a tube that’s open at both ends has a fundamental frequency of 120 Hz, what is the frequency of its third overtone?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"Since we already know the value of the fundamental frequency (n = 1), we can solve for the third overtone (n = 4) using the equation \\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{v}{2L}\\) .","solution":"Since fundamental frequency (n = 1) is\n\\({f}_{1} = \\frac{v}{2L}\\text{,}\\)\nand\n\\({f}_{4} = 4\\frac{v}{2L}\\text{,}{f}_{4} = 4{f}_{1} = 4(\\text{120 Hz)=480 Hz}\\text{.}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"To solve this problem, it wasn’t necessary to know the length of the tube or the speed of the air because of the relationship between the fundamental and the third overtone. This example was of an open-pipe resonator; note that for a closed-pipe resonator, the third overtone has a value of n = 7 (not n = 4)."},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.ex03","number":"14.4.3","title":"Using Beat Frequency to Tune a Piano","problem":"Piano tuners use beats routinely in their work. When comparing a note with a tuning fork, they listen for beats and adjust the string until the beats go away (to zero frequency). If a piano tuner hears two beats per second, and the tuning fork has a frequency of 256 Hz, what are the possible frequencies of the piano?","problemImages":[],"strategy":"Since we already know that the beat frequency f B is 2, and one of the frequencies (let’s say f 2 ) is 256 Hz, we can use the equation \\({f}_{B} = |{f}_{1} - {f}_{2}|\\) to solve for the frequency of the piano f 1 .","solution":"Since \\({f}_{B} = |{f}_{1} - {f}_{2}|\\),\nwe know that either \\({f}_{B} = {f}_{1} - {f}_{2}\\) or \\(- {f}_{B} = {f}_{1} - {f}_{2}\\) .\nSolving for f 1 ,\n\\({f}_{1} = {f}_{B} + {f}_{2}\\text{or}{f}_{1} = - {f}_{B} + {f}_{2}.\\)\nSubstituting in values,\n\\({f}_{1} = 2 + 256\\,\\text{Hz or}{f}_{1}\\text{=}-2 + 256\\,\\text{Hz}\\)\nSo,\n\\({f}_{1} = 258\\,\\text{Hz or 254 Hz.}\\)","solutionImages":[],"solutionTables":[],"discussion":"The piano tuner might not initially be able to tell simply by listening whether the frequency of the piano is too high or too low and must tune it by trial and error, making an adjustment and then testing it again. If there are even more beats after the adjustment, then the tuner knows that he went in the wrong direction."}],"snapLabs":[],"tipsAndFeatures":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.feat01","type":"tip","title":"Tips for Success","text":"Don’t confuse the beat frequency with the regular frequency of a wave resulting from superposition. While the beat frequency is given by the formula above, and describes the frequency of the beats, the actual frequency of the wave resulting from superposition is the average of the frequencies of the two original waves.","images":[]}],"summaryPoints":["A system’s natural frequency is the frequency at which the system will oscillate if not affected by driving or damping forces.","A periodic force driving a harmonic oscillator at its natural frequency produces resonance. The system is said to resonate.","Beats occur when waves of slightly different frequencies are superimposed.","In air columns, the lowest-frequency resonance is called the fundamental, whereas all higher resonant frequencies are called overtones. Collectively, they are called harmonics.","The resonant frequencies of a tube closed at one end are \\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{v}{4L},n = 1,3,5...\\), where f 1 is the fundamental and L is the length of the tube.","The resonant frequencies of a tube open at both ends are \\({f}_{n} = n\\frac{v}{2L},n = 1,2,3...\\)"],"glossary":[{"term":"beat","termVi":"Phách âm học","definition":"a phenomenon produced by the superposition of two waves with slightly different frequencies but the same amplitude","definitionVi":"Sự biến thiên to – nhỏ tuần hoàn do giao thoa của hai sóng âm có tần số gần bằng nhau.","memoryTipVi":"Thợ lên dây đàn guitar dựa vào hiện tượng phách."},{"term":"beat frequency","termVi":"Tần số phách (f_B)","definition":"the frequency of the amplitude fluctuations of a wave","definitionVi":"Hiệu độ lớn hai tần số: f_B = |f₁ - f₂|.","memoryTipVi":"Khi 2 dây đàn chuẩn cùng tần số thì f_B = 0 (hết phách)."},{"term":"damping","termVi":"Sự tắt dần (của dao động)","definition":"the reduction in amplitude over time as the energy of an oscillation dissipates","definitionVi":"Sự giảm dần biên độ dao động do ma sát hoặc lực cản môi trường.","memoryTipVi":"Bộ giảm xóc ô tô dùng cơ chế damping."},{"term":"fundamental","termVi":"Âm cơ bản (Họa âm bậc 1, f₁)","definition":"the lowest-frequency resonance","definitionVi":"Tần số cộng hưởng thấp nhất của một dây đàn hoặc ống sáo.","memoryTipVi":"Quyết định nốt nhạc chính mà tai ta nghe thấy."},{"term":"harmonics","termVi":"Các họa âm (f_n = n·f₁)","definition":"the term used to refer to the fundamental and its overtones","definitionVi":"Tập hợp các tần số cộng hưởng bội nguyên của âm cơ bản (tạo nên âm sắc riêng của từng nhạc cụ).","memoryTipVi":"Giúp phân biệt tiếng piano và tiếng violin dù chơi cùng một nốt La!"},{"term":"natural frequency","termVi":"Tần số dao động riêng (f₀)","definition":"the frequency at which a system would oscillate if there were no driving and no damping forces","definitionVi":"Tần số mà vật tự dao động khi bị kích thích rồi thả tự do.","memoryTipVi":"Phụ thuộc cấu tạo riêng của hệ."},{"term":"overtones","termVi":"Các họa âm bậc cao (từ f₂ trở lên)","definition":"all resonant frequencies higher than the fundamental","definitionVi":"Tất cả các tần số cộng hưởng cao hơn âm cơ bản.","memoryTipVi":"Overtone thứ nhất chính là Harmonic thứ hai (đối với ống hở 2 đầu/dây đàn)."},{"term":"resonance","termVi":"Hiện tượng cộng hưởng","definition":"the phenomenon of driving a system with a frequency equal to the system's natural frequency","definitionVi":"Biên độ dao động cưỡng bức tăng vọt cực đại khi tần số ngoại lực bằng đúng tần số riêng của hệ.","memoryTipVi":"Ứng dụng trong hộp cộng hưởng đàn guitar, lò vi sóng và chụp MRI."},{"term":"resonate","termVi":"Dao động cộng hưởng","definition":"to drive a system at its natural frequency","definitionVi":"Động từ chỉ việc một hệ dao động mạnh lên do hiện tượng cộng hưởng.","memoryTipVi":"Resonance (danh từ) → Resonate (động từ)."}],"assessments":[{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q003","sourceId":"fs-id1167064824915","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"In the tuning fork and tube experiment, in case a standing wave is formed, at what point on the tube is the maximum disturbance from the tuning fork observed? Recall that the tube has one open end and one closed end.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"At the midpoint of the tube"},{"label":"b","text":"Both ends of the tube"},{"label":"c","text":"At the closed end of the tube"},{"label":"d","text":"At the open end of the tube"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q004","sourceId":"fs-id1167066057430","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"In the tuning fork and tube experiment, when will the air column produce the loudest sound?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"If the tuning fork vibrates at a frequency twice that of the natural frequency of the air column."},{"label":"b","text":"If the tuning fork vibrates at a frequency lower than the natural frequency of the air column."},{"label":"c","text":"If the tuning fork vibrates at a frequency higher than the natural frequency of the air column."},{"label":"d","text":"If the tuning fork vibrates at a frequency equal to the natural frequency of the air column."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q005","sourceId":"fs-id1167065877567","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What is a closed-pipe resonator?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"A pipe or cylindrical air column closed at both ends"},{"label":"b","text":"A pipe with an antinode at the closed end"},{"label":"c","text":"A pipe with a node at the open end"},{"label":"d","text":"A pipe or cylindrical air column closed at one end"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q006","sourceId":"fs-id1167065033167","category":"check-understanding","categoryLabel":"Check Your Understanding (Kiểm tra đọc hiểu)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Give two examples of open-pipe resonators.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"piano, violin"},{"label":"b","text":"drum, tabla"},{"label":"c","text":"rlectric guitar, acoustic guitar"},{"label":"d","text":"flute, oboe"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q009","sourceId":"fs-id1167065938369","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"When does a yo-yo travel the farthest from the finger?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"when the amplitude of the finger moving up and down is greater than the amplitude of the yo-yo"},{"label":"b","text":"when the amplitude of the finger moving up and down is less than the amplitude of the yo-yo"},{"label":"c","text":"when the frequency of the finger moving up and down is equal to the resonant frequency of the yo-yo"},{"label":"d","text":"when the frequency of the finger moving up and down is different from the resonant frequency of the yo-yo"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q011","sourceId":"fs-id1167065825298","category":"concept","categoryLabel":"Concept Items (Câu hỏi khái niệm)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"What kind of waves form in pipe resonators?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"damped waves"},{"label":"b","text":"propagating waves"},{"label":"c","text":"high-frequency waves"},{"label":"d","text":"standing waves"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q013","sourceId":"fs-id1167065972888","category":"critical-thinking","categoryLabel":"Critical Thinking (Tư duy phản biện)","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"When does the amplitude of an oscillating system become maximum?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"When two sound waves interfere destructively."},{"label":"b","text":"When the driving force produces a transverse wave in the system."},{"label":"c","text":"When the driving force of the oscillator to the oscillating system is at a maximum amplitude."},{"label":"d","text":"When the frequency of the oscillator equals the natural frequency of the oscillating system."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q020","sourceId":"fs-id1167065847442","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Observation of which phenomenon can be considered proof that something is a wave?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"interference"},{"label":"b","text":"noise"},{"label":"c","text":"reflection"},{"label":"d","text":"conduction"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q021","sourceId":"fs-id1167064649316","category":"test-prep-mc","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trắc nghiệm","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"Which of the resonant frequencies has the greatest amplitude?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"The first harmonic"},{"label":"b","text":"The second harmonic"},{"label":"c","text":"The first overtone"},{"label":"d","text":"The second overtone"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q026","sourceId":"fs-id1167062950036","category":"test-prep-sa","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Trả lời ngắn","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"How is human speech produced?","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"Human speech is produced by shaping the cavity formed by the throat and mouth, the vibration of vocal cords, and using the tongue to adjust the\nfundamental frequency and combination of overtones."},{"label":"b","text":"Human speech is produced by shaping the cavity formed by the throat and mouth into a closed pipe and using tongue to adjust the fundamental frequency and combination of overtones."},{"label":"c","text":"Human speech is produced only by the vibrations of the tongue."},{"label":"d","text":"Human speech is produced by elongating the vocal cords."}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null},{"uid":"hsphys.ch14.sec04.q034","sourceId":"fs-id1167066165997","category":"test-prep-er","categoryLabel":"Test Prep — Tự luận mở rộng","isMultipleChoice":true,"prompt":"True or false—An open-pipe resonator has more overtones than a closed-pipe resonator.","images":[],"choices":[{"label":"a","text":"False"},{"label":"b","text":"True"}],"hasOfficialSolution":false,"solution":null}],"stats":{"learningObjectiveCount":4,"keyEquationCount":0,"subsectionCount":4,"workedExampleCount":3,"snapLabCount":0,"glossaryCount":9,"assessmentCount":11}}],"summary":{"totalSections":5,"totalKeyEquations":1,"totalWorkedExamples":7,"totalSnapLabs":2,"totalGlossaryTerms":20,"totalAssessments":43,"referencedMediaCount":27},"qualityGate":{"passed":true,"checkedUids":71,"errors":[],"warnings":[]}};
